  "title": "Week 42: I Am Low IQ for the First Time 🤯",
  "date": "2025-10-10T21:00:00.000Z",
  "content": "Week 42: I Am Low IQ for the First Time 🤯\n\nThe Dream Team That Couldn’t\n\nThis summer, I got an “internship offer” from a group who claimed they would revolutionize hiring.\nAfter a few chats, I realized they might actually not worked once:\n\nno experience ✅\n\nno skills ✅\n\nno money ✅\n\n...but a big idea to change the world 🌍\n\nSo I politely refused. (And by “politely,” I mean I ran.)\n\nThe Test That Tested My Faith in Humanity\n\nFast forward a few weeks.\nAnother company reached out — the one that asked for my 20-year-old high school transcript (yes, really).\nThey sent me a link to the Thomas GIA Test.\n\nI thought, “Sure, why not?”\nThen I opened it.\nIt was close to purple 💜...\n\nClearly not AI-generated, because no AI would dare produce such terrible UX.\nThe test looked like something between kindergarten worksheets and gorilla enrichment puzzles:\n\n“Wang is taller than Zhang. Who is shorter?”\n\n“Find how many characters are pronounced the same.”\n\n\n“Find the furthest number.”\n\n\nThey even asked me to use my native language for accuracy.\nWhich is cute — except their “translation” was so bad I had to translate it back into English to understand what it meant. 🤦‍♂️\n\nThe Humiliation\n\nI was bored.\nI’m a programmer. I can do calculus in my head.\nThese questions were so simple they made me question their IQ, not mine.\n\nThen... the twist.\nThat afternoon, I got an email:\n\n“We regret to inform you... you did not pass the test.”\n\nWait. WHAT?! 😳\n\nMy “Low IQ” Report\n\nI downloaded my “results.”\nThere are five test areas:\n\nPerceptual Speed\n\nReasoning\n\nNumber Speed & Accuracy\n\nSpatial Visualization\n\nWord Meaning\n\nI scored above average in four — but below in one.\nGuess which?\n“Word Meaning.”\n\nSo apparently, I failed because I couldn’t understand their bad Chinese translations.\nHonestly, that’s fair. Even Google Translate would cry over that mess. 😂\n\nThe Rabbit Hole 🕳️\n\nBeing a Digital Learning Game student, I got curious.\nHow did this “test” even come to exist?\n\nTurns out it was born in the late 1980s, a commercial adaptation of the British Army Recruitment Battery (BARB).\nYes — it was literally a military IQ test turned corporate HR tool.\n\nEven more shocking?\nNo scientific paper, no peer-reviewed journal, and no transparency in 50 years.\nEven the MBTI (the astrology of psychology) has more research behind it! 🧠✨\n\nCultural Mismatch Madness\n\nThe test proudly claims to be “localized.”\nLet’s see:\n\nIn Chinese, testers count characters with the same pronunciation.\n\n\nIn English, testers find capitalized letters.\n\n\nTotally equal, right? 😅\n(52 English letters vs. literally millions of Chinese characters.)\n\nThey did not separate the data with languages. Surely I am average in the other 4 tests among my home people.\n\nHR’s Favorite Toy 🧩\n\nDespite this, many HRs swear by the GIA test.\nThey call it “scientific.” I call it pseudo-science wrapped in corporate purple.\n\nIf they really believe this measures intelligence, then maybe it’s actually a loyalty test —\nto find the people who will do anything without questioning the boss. 🙇‍♂️\n\nFinal Thoughts 💭\n\nThey bragged about having “80,000+ applicants every month.”\n(Why does every company love this number? It’s not even that impressive.)\n\nEurope once led the world in logic and innovation.\nNow it’s letting unscientific, outdated IQ tests decide who gets hired.\n\nIf this is the new standard for intelligence,\nthen I’m proud to say —\nI’m officially low IQ. 🥳",
